WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.884
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level four.

2
00:00:06.184 --> 00:00:09.605
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.905 --> 00:00:15.809
Unit one: Geopolitics. Today you will highlight key causes and actors with it-cleft sentences.

4
00:00:16.109 --> 00:00:18.633
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.567 --> 00:00:22.800
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:24.779
Geopolitical.

7
00:00:25.783 --> 00:00:31.185
Geopolitical means relating to how geography and politics affect power between countries.

8
00:00:31.783 --> 00:00:34.572
Geopolitical rivalry shaped the whole century.

9
00:00:35.183 --> 00:00:37.720
Your turn. Say: Geopolitical.

10
00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:41.858
Geopolitical.

11
00:00:42.617 --> 00:00:44.105
Sphere of influence.

12
00:00:45.117 --> 00:00:49.647
Sphere of influence means an area where one country has strong political control.

13
00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:52.842
The empire protected its sphere of influence.

14
00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:56.799
Your turn. Say: Sphere of influence.

15
00:00:59.633 --> 00:01:01.819
Sphere of influence.

16
00:01:02.583 --> 00:01:04.246
Strategic interest.

17
00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:09.664
Strategic interest means something a country must protect to stay safe or powerful.

18
00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:12.911
Secure trade routes are a strategic interest.

19
00:01:13.517 --> 00:01:17.133
Your turn. Say: Strategic interest.

20
00:01:19.967 --> 00:01:21.699
Strategic interest.

21
00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:23.602
Border dispute.

22
00:01:24.617 --> 00:01:28.915
Border dispute means a disagreement between countries about where a frontier lies.

23
00:01:29.517 --> 00:01:32.108
A border dispute closed the mountain crossing.

24
00:01:32.717 --> 00:01:36.100
Your turn. Say: Border dispute.

25
00:01:38.967 --> 00:01:40.270
Border dispute.

26
00:01:41.017 --> 00:01:42.238
Power balance.

27
00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:47.781
Power balance means a situation in which no country is strong enough to dominate.

28
00:01:48.383 --> 00:01:50.708
The power balance kept the peace for decades.

29
00:01:51.317 --> 00:01:54.224
Your turn. Say: Power balance.

30
00:01:57.067 --> 00:01:58.370
Power balance.

31
00:01:59.117 --> 00:02:00.408
Regional stability.

32
00:02:01.417 --> 00:02:06.237
Regional stability means a calm situation in which a whole area stays peaceful.

33
00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:09.755
Regional stability attracts foreign investment.

34
00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:13.374
Your turn. Say: Regional stability.

35
00:02:16.233 --> 00:02:17.768
Regional stability.

36
00:02:18.517 --> 00:02:19.680
Superpower.

37
00:02:20.683 --> 00:02:25.133
Superpower means a country with enormous military and economic strength.

38
00:02:25.717 --> 00:02:28.378
Each superpower tried to win new allies.

39
00:02:28.983 --> 00:02:31.566
Your turn. Say: Superpower.

40
00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:35.366
Superpower.

41
00:02:36.117 --> 00:02:37.164
Alliance.

42
00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:41.624
Alliance means an agreement between countries to support each other.

43
00:02:42.217 --> 00:02:44.681
The alliance promised to defend every member.

44
00:02:45.283 --> 00:02:47.912
Your turn. Say: Alliance.

45
00:02:50.767 --> 00:02:51.965
Alliance.

46
00:02:52.767 --> 00:02:56.568
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:57.417 --> 00:03:05.421
Visal, an analyst writes 'It is the balance of power that keeps the region stable.' Why not simply say 'The balance of power keeps the region stable'?

48
00:03:06.517 --> 00:03:19.354
The it-cleft puts focus on one part. It is + focused element + that/who + the rest. It shows exactly what matters: it is the alliance that deters attack.

49
00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:22.798
Can we focus on time or place too?

50
00:03:23.883 --> 00:03:39.353
Yes: 'It was in 1991 that the superpower collapsed' or 'It is in border areas that disputes begin'. For people we can use who: It was the leaders who refused.

51
00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:42.066
When do journalists use it?

52
00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:54.243
To correct a belief or stress a cause: 'It is not the army but the alliance that protects them.' It adds emphasis without changing the facts.

53
00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:57.040
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:57.883 --> 00:04:02.356
Grammar focus: It-cleft: It is plus focus plus that and who.

55
00:04:02.656 --> 00:04:03.959
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:07.613
It is the balance of power that keeps the region stable.

57
00:04:08.517 --> 00:04:12.397
It was in 1991 that the superpower collapsed.

58
00:04:13.283 --> 00:04:15.782
It was the leaders who refused to compromise.

59
00:04:16.733 --> 00:04:24.099
To stress one part, use It is / was + the focused part + that (who for people). It adds emphasis only, not new information.

60
00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:29.534
Be careful! Don't say: It was in 1991 what the state fell.

61
00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:34.916
Say: It was in 1991 that the state fell.

62
00:04:35.216 --> 00:04:37.471
In a cleft, use 'that' (or 'who' for people).

63
00:04:38.267 --> 00:04:40.791
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:42.915
Why Borders Matter.

65
00:04:43.215 --> 00:04:46.700
Geopolitical analysts study how geography shapes power.

66
00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:51.963
It is often a border dispute that turns a quiet rivalry into a crisis.

67
00:04:52.967 --> 00:04:58.763
A superpower defends its sphere of influence because its strategic interest depends on friendly neighbours.

68
00:04:59.767 --> 00:05:04.425
It is the power balance, rather than goodwill, that usually preserves regional stability.

69
00:05:05.433 --> 00:05:10.614
Alliances matter for the same reason: it is collective strength that discourages aggression.

70
00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:15.597
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:16.450 --> 00:05:18.717
It was the leaders blank refused to talk.

72
00:05:19.017 --> 00:05:22.368
A, who. B, which. C, what.

73
00:05:25.933 --> 00:05:27.770
The answer is A: who.

74
00:05:28.917 --> 00:05:32.449
A group of countries that agree to defend each other is an blank.

75
00:05:32.749 --> 00:05:37.110
A, border dispute. B, alliance. C, superpower.

76
00:05:40.667 --> 00:05:42.771
The answer is B: alliance.

77
00:05:43.917 --> 00:05:47.971
The area where a powerful country has most control is its sphere of blank.

78
00:05:48.271 --> 00:05:52.180
A, stability. B, interest. C, influence.

79
00:05:55.733 --> 00:05:58.232
The answer is C: influence.

80
00:05:59.383 --> 00:06:01.522
Choose the correct cleft sentence.

81
00:06:01.822 --> 00:06:08.726
A, It the treaty that ended the war.. B, It was the treaty that ended the war.. C, Was it the treaty ended the war..

82
00:06:12.267 --> 00:06:15.508
The answer is B: It was the treaty that ended the war..

83
00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:20.345
According to the reading, what usually preserves regional stability?

84
00:06:20.645 --> 00:06:24.995
A, goodwill. B, the power balance. C, a border dispute.

85
00:06:28.533 --> 00:06:31.229
The answer is B: the power balance.

86
00:06:32.433 --> 00:06:35.480
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:36.317 --> 00:06:39.767
How can a border dispute affect an entire region's stability?

88
00:06:48.583 --> 00:06:49.979
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:50.279 --> 00:07:15.716
A border dispute can weaken regional stability because neighbouring countries often take sides. Trade routes may close, armies may gather and refugees may cross the frontier. Other powers, such as a superpower or an alliance, may become involved, which raises the risk of a wider conflict. Clear diplomacy and agreements on the border can calm the situation before it spreads.

90
00:07:16.016 --> 00:07:17.771
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:18.683 --> 00:07:24.923
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and it-cleft: it is plus focus plus that and who.

92
00:07:25.223 --> 00:07:30.325
Today's value is global perspective. Local stories can carry universal truths.

93
00:07:30.625 --> 00:07:33.231
See you in Unit two. Goodbye!

94
00:07:33.531 --> 00:07:36.474
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

